  "news": [
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "Oil rally keeps bonds under strain",
      "summary": "Stocks and futures slipped as elevated crude prices pressured Treasuries, pushing the 10-year yield to levels last seen roughly 19 years ago. Brent climbed toward $107 and up, capping a September gain of about 18%. The dollar advanced and Bitcoin weakened as hopes for near-term US crypto regulation slipped. Risk appetite stayed checked heading into the Fed meeting."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "The 10-year yield crossed 5% and a rate hike looms",
      "summary": "Futures declined as surging oil fed inflation worries and the 10-year Treasury yield hit about 5.03%. Markets are pricing better than 90% odds of a quarter-point Fed hike on Wednesday, which would take the target range to 3.75-4.00%. Brent sat near $108-109 and WTI near $104 on Gulf attacks and the Saudi pipeline shutdown, after a Monday session led lower by chipmakers."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "10-year at its highest since 2007 as crude extends gains",
      "summary": "Futures pointed lower with the 10-year above 5.04% in early trade and crude still climbing on Middle East fighting. Major indexes closed lower Monday, the fifth decline in six sessions, as chipmakers sank on AI safety warnings. Traders continue to see high odds of a rate hike Wednesday."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stocks",
      "title": "Global shares mostly lower after AI stocks sink",
      "summary": "Asian and European markets traded lower following Wall Street's weakness in AI and chip names. SoftBank recovered part of its losses in Tokyo after comments about a delay to an OpenAI listing. US futures stayed under pressure through the overnight session."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stocks",
      "title": "AI slowdown fears hit chipmakers while cybersecurity jumps",
      "summary": "US stocks ended Monday lower with Nvidia and other chipmakers leading the declines, after AI executives raised safety concerns and urged a slower pace of frontier model development. CrowdStrike, Palo Alto Networks and their peers went the other way, surging on relative resilience and analyst support."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Crypto",
      "title": "Senate votes today on the Clarity Act after the final text drop",
      "summary": "Republicans released the last draft Monday with 126 Democratic changes included. A cloture vote on the motion to proceed is set for this afternoon, around 2:15pm ET. That vote only starts debate rather than deciding the bill, so it is not a law-or-no-law moment. If cloture fails, the path for the rest of 2026 gets much thinner."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Crypto",
      "title": "Trump accepted tougher crypto ethics rules",
      "summary": "Under the agreed terms, covered officials cannot issue or sponsor a digital asset, and must divest a significant crypto interest or place it in a qualified blind trust. State attorneys general get enforcement power. That package was the Democratic blocker, and prediction-market odds jumped on the deal before fading again overnight."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Crypto",
      "title": "Crypto proxies ripped Monday, then sold off Tuesday premarket",
      "summary": "CRCL, COIN, MSTR and HOOD all faded between 3% and 6% premarket after Monday's bounce on the ethics deal, and Bitcoin gave back its spike above $79k. That is the classic pattern of buying the headline and then selling the 60-vote math, since passage this year still has to clear the Senate and a House that has already left town."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Crypto",
      "title": "What the bill would actually do to the proxies",
      "summary": "Clarity would split digital commodities under the CFTC from securities under the SEC, give exchanges and custodians a real rulebook, and add a Treasury kill-switch on stablecoin rewards if deposits flee community banks. That is why CRCL and COIN move more on the vote than MSTR does, since MSTR still tracks Bitcoin first. SEC Chair Atkins has said the agency will keep writing crypto rules with or without the bill."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Crypto",
      "title": "The odds are not a done deal",
      "summary": "Passage needs roughly seven to nine Democratic votes plus GOP holdouts. TD Cowen still puts enactment this year at about 25%, and even a Senate win likely waits on a lame-duck House. If cloture fails the complex fades; if it passes, COIN, CRCL and HOOD can rip again into the next round of procedural votes."
    }
  ],
